== Independence Permutation Test
<apx-interpost-independence>

The temptation the ECDF decision had to resist: the global quantity is easy to model and the within one is not, so derive the within from the global. If posts were generated by a single session-blind process, within gaps would be the global gaps that happen to fall inside session windows, and the simulator would only need the global model plus the session gate it already has (the staleness rule drops creates that land past session end). One distribution instead of five, zero new code. This appendix shows the derivation fails ---with an exact permutation test and two descriptive replays--- and that the failure is informative: it rules out not just the naive gate but also the natural parametric repair, leaving the ECDF of @sec-cal-interpost-ecdf as the justified rather than merely convenient choice.

=== Hypotheses and Test Statistic

Let ${T_i}$ be a user's post times and ${[S_j, E_j]}$ their session windows. H0 (independence): the post process is independent of the window process ---given the gap sequence, the alignment between posts and windows is uniform; windows observe the stream, they do not modulate it. H1: posting is coupled to session state (rate modulation). The test statistic is the number of within-session pairs, $N = sum_i bb(1)[exists j: S_j <= T_i < T_(i+1) <= E_j]$, one-sided (H1 predicts $N_"obs" > N_"null"$).

A parametric test was deliberately avoided: any likelihood-based test needs a model of the post process under H0, which is heavy-tailed, user-specific and serially correlated (burstiness). A wrong null model would confound "the model is wrong" with "the coupling exists". A permutation test needs no model of the gaps at all: it conditions on the user's _observed_ gap sequence and randomizes only the quantity H0 says is irrelevant ---the alignment.

=== The Null Ensemble and the Result

Under H0, given the ordered gap sequence and the window layout, the time origin of the stream relative to the windows is uniform. The exact randomization is therefore a circular time-shift: wrap the real span into a circle and rotate by $U tilde "Uniform"(0, t_n - t_0)$. This preserves _everything_ about the user's behavior ---every gap value, their order, all autocorrelation and burst structure, the window layout--- and destroys only the post↔window alignment. It is the maximal preserving randomization: any residual difference must be attributed to the alignment itself. (An iid bootstrap of the gaps would also destroy serial structure, leaving the test open to the objection that burstiness, not alignment, drove the rejection; it is retained below only as a probe.)

Result (1,000 users sampled from the global $n_"obs" >= 30$ pool; 920 with at least 5 real within pairs enter the test; $R = 999$ shifts each):

