The rejection is not marginal: for essentially every user, _no_ random alignment of their own posting rhythm comes anywhere near the observed within-session density. Posting is coupled to session state.

=== Decomposition Probes

The test establishes _that_ the alignment matters; two descriptive replays establish _how_. They are not part of the test and carry no p-values; they guide the modeling decision (@fig-cal-interpost-independence).

- *iid replay --- rate, not shape.* Bootstrap the user's own empirical global gaps into a fresh stream (same span, t0 = first real post) and replay against the real windows. Count: median real/synth ratio 19 times, and synthetic reaches at least 5 within pairs for only 9--11% of users. Shape: among gate-surviving pairs, per-user median(real)/median(synth) = 1.02, p75 identical. Within gaps are not specially _short_; they simply occur far more often while inside a session. Modulation is in rate, not shape.
- *conditional-truncation replay --- the smart repair, rejected.* Keep the global model but inside each session sample gaps conditioned on fitting the remaining session time, walking the session from its start. The repair overshoots: median real/cond ratio 0.3 times (~3 times too many within pairs), and the synthetic gaps are too short (pooled medians 49 s vs 95 s; per-user ratio 2.03). Conditioning a heavy-tailed law on $x <=$ (small remaining time) concentrates mass on tiny gaps, and re-conditioning on the _shrinking_ remaining time drifts successive draws shorter still ---systematically shorter than anything real in-session behavior produces.

The verdict (count and shape relative to the real within distribution):

