=== The Closed Routes

All three cheap routes are now closed:

1. *Session-independent posts* (sample global, let the gate produce within) ---disproved by the permutation test above.
2. *Conditional-truncation sampling* from the global fits ---disproved by the second probe.
3. *Truncated-likelihood parametric fits* of the within quantity (e.g. fitdistrplus + truncdist) ---statistically valid in principle, but the truncation point varies per pair (remaining session time, not a fixed bound), demanding a custom likelihood, and estimates remain high-variance in the regime of @apx-interpost-truncated (ceiling on the bulk; light families separable only at $T >= 900$ s while within p90 = 281 s). Out of scope.

Hence the ECDF sampling of @sec-cal-interpost-ecdf, chosen by elimination rather than by convenience.

=== Ontology Note

Neither test nor probes privilege the global quantity as the "natural" process. Users can only post while online, so the generative decomposition runs the other way: global gaps are a two-component mixture ---within-session gaps and cross-session spans (session remainder + offline wait + next-session offset)--- and the dominant lognormal global fits are single families stretched over that mixture, compressions rather than behavioral laws. The simulator reflects the correct direction: sessions and within cadence are the two generative components, and the global distribution survives only as a user-level heterogeneity label selecting which per-family ECDF to load. The global counterfactual was nevertheless indispensable: only by rejecting the session-blind model can the two-component design be justified over the cheaper one-component one.

=== Threats to Validity

- *Window--post circularity.* Sessions are built from all events _including_ posts, so windows and post times are coupled by construction. Likes dominate events ~5:1 over posts, so windows are mostly like-defined; the shift null preserves windows, gaps and order, so the rejection isolates the alignment, not window formation.
- *Resolution floor.* With $R = 999$ the smallest reportable p is 0.001; the true per-user p-values are likely far smaller (the median null count is 21 times below observed).
- *User pool.* The test conditions on >= 30 global gaps and >= 5 within pairs ---active users. This is the population that matters for the simulator's posting dynamics; casual users contribute few posts either way.
- *Span wrap.* The circular shift treats the observed span as a torus; for users whose span is dominated by one long gap, a shift can concentrate posts artificially. This affects the null, is shared across users, and would have to systematically deplete within pairs 21-fold to explain the result ---implausible, and the iid probe (no wrap) shows the same deficit.

== Pooled Parametric Fits
<apx-interpost-pooledfit>

Per-user fits are unidentifiable (Finding 1, @apx-interpost-truncated) and the permutation test closed the derivation routes (@apx-interpost-independence); the chosen route is ECDF sampling. One question remains open: can _one_ distribution with _one_ parameter set describe the within-session gaps of a whole user group? Two groupings were tried (`pooled_fit.py`, same 8-candidate MLE battery and closed-form KS/CvM/AD as @apx-method-gof-dist, scipy instead of fitdistrplus): everyone pooled (1M uniform subsample of all 11.1M within gaps) and per (duration $times$ gap) pair ---users grouped by their settled parametric type pair, within gaps pooled per pair (250k gaps each).

=== Everyone Pooled

@tbl-apx-interpost-pooled shows the result on the full pool. The AIC winner is gamma ---a light tail--- and the heavy-tail look of the pooled histogram is a mixture artifact: pooling heterogeneous users manufactures an apparent heavy tail that no single heavy-tailed family fits (lognorm and fisk lose by more than $150,000$ AIC; pareto degenerates).

